# Docker Workflows: Dev vs Prod

This document outlines how your Cloud Drive application runs in both Development and Production, including architecture flowcharts and the exact commands to manage them.

---

## 🛠️ Development Workflow

In development, we use Docker for the backend services, but keep the Vite React frontend running locally on your Mac. This gives you instant hot-reloading for UI work while keeping the API and Database fully containerized.

```mermaid
graph TD
    Developer([👨‍💻 You])
    
    subgraph "Local Mac (Outside Docker)"
        Vite[⚡ Vite Dev Server\nPort: 3000\nHot Reload Enabled]
    end
    
    subgraph "🐳 docker-compose.dev.yml"
        Server[🟢 Node API\nPort: 9000\nBind-mounted code]
        Mongo[(🍃 MongoDB\nPort: 27017)]
        MongoExpress[📊 Mongo Express UI\nPort: 8081]
    end
    
    LocalVolume[(📁 Local Dev Storage\n./data/storage)]
    
    Developer -->|React Coding| Vite
    Developer -->|View Database| MongoExpress
    Vite -->|Proxy /api/v1| Server
    Server -->|Reads/Writes| Mongo
    Server -->|File I/O| LocalVolume
```

### Development Commands

> **Important**: The dev stack requires [apps/server/env/.env.development](file:///Users/deepakram/Personal/cloud-drive/apps/server/env/.env.development) to exist.

**Start the Stack:**
```bash
# 1. Start the Docker Backend (Mongo, Express Server)
docker-compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.dev.yml --env-file .env.dev up --build -d

# 2. Start the Frontend (in a separate terminal)
npm run web:dev
```

**Stop the Stack:**
```bash
docker-compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.dev.yml down
```

**View Logs:**
```bash
docker-compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.dev.yml --env-file .env.dev logs -f server
```

---

## 🚀 Production Workflow

In production, the entire application is containerized. The [web](file:///Users/deepakram/Personal/cloud-drive/Dockerfile.web) container builds the React app into static files and serves them via Nginx. The [server](file:///Users/deepakram/Personal/cloud-drive/Dockerfile.server) connects to your remote MongoDB Atlas cluster and saves files directly to your connected USB PenDrive.

```mermaid
graph TD
    User([🌍 End User])
    
    subgraph "🐳 docker-compose.prod.yml"
        Nginx[🌐 Nginx Web Server\nPort: 80\nServes Static React Build]
        Server[🟢 Node API\nPort: 9000]
    end
    
    Atlas[(☁️ MongoDB Atlas\nRemote Cluster)]
    Pendrive[(💾 Physical PenDrive\n/Volumes/PenDrive/cloud-drive-storage)]
    
    User -->|Accesses UI| Nginx
    Nginx -->|Proxy /api/v1| Server
    Server -->|Reads/Writes| Atlas
    Server -->|File I/O| Pendrive
```

### Production Commands

> **Important**: The prod stack requires [apps/server/env/.env.production](file:///Users/deepakram/Personal/cloud-drive/apps/server/env/.env.production) to exist with real credentials.

**Start the Stack (Pointing to PenDrive):**
```bash
# Starts the stack and mounts the physical PenDrive
docker-compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.prod.yml --env-file .env.prod up --build -d
```

**Stop the Stack:**
```bash
docker-compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.prod.yml down
```

**View Logs:**
```bash
docker-compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.prod.yml --env-file .env.prod logs -f server
docker-compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.prod.yml --env-file .env.prod logs -f web
```

**Rebuild the Backend or Frontend (e.g. after pushing new code):**
```bash
docker-compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.prod.yml --env-file .env.prod build --no-cache
docker-compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.prod.yml --env-file .env.prod up -d
```

---

## 🧹 Docker Cleanup Commands

If Docker ever starts acting weird or throwing cache errors, use these commands:

```bash
# Total wipe of all unused containers, networks, images (cache)
docker system prune -a --volumes

# View running docker processes
docker ps

# Restart the docker daemon (Mac terminal)
killall Docker && open /Applications/Docker.app
```
